multiview-stage5.patch — layered EGLImage import, depth array, pass-break counter, load-action probe, frame GPU time
==================================================================================
(stage 5.0a of the multiview plan of the Vice City port — GENERIC, no game
assumptions; usable by any port that wants to render a Metal array texture as
GL_TEXTURE_2D_ARRAY)

Base:        ANGLE e4499e6b2835a6996507f1b99920bc56f0122573
Order:       apply after klepton, angle-metal-fixes, multiview-stage2/3/4.
State:       5.0a (2026-09-25), verified on the HOST (macOS Metal, draw +
             readback via Metal blit): imported private 2D array + GL-owned
             D24S8 array, 2x MSAA, 2 views -> COMPLETE; the depth test works
             in both layers; foveation BY IDENTITY on the imported Metal
             object applies directly. Confirmed on the device (Vision Pro M5,
             visionOS 26.6) 2026-09-25: identical measurements.
             Addendum 5.0b/4: building block 4 (load-action probe).

Five building blocks:

1. GL_EXT_EGL_image_array in the Metal backend (DisplayMtl advertises it;
   the frontend validation already existed). ImageMtl: a 2D-array MTLTexture
   imported WITHOUT EGL_METAL_TEXTURE_ARRAY_SLICE_ANGLE stays an array
   (gl::TextureType::_2DArray) and reports arrayLength as depth; WITH the
   attribute it is a slice as before. TextureMtl::setEGLImageTarget accepts
   the array type and offers a fallback for old importers: an array image
   bound to GL_TEXTURE_2D = slice 0 (the previous default behaviour).
   Result: register the Klepton rate map by identity on the array texture,
   put a GL array object on it, multiview FBO — no slice view, no detour.
2. Depth/stencil as a memoryless MS array: no code needed — the 4b path
   (MakeMemoryLess2DMSArrayTexture) supports depth formats; PROVEN here
   (D24S8 array via glFramebufferTextureMultisampleMultiviewOVR on
   GL_DEPTH_STENCIL_ATTACHMENT, depth test correct per layer).
3. Pass-break counter (KL_ANGLE_VRR_TRACE): counts per frame how often a
   rate-mapped or layered pass was ended with "save content" (= it continues
   with Load; under a rate map that is the double-distortion path).
   Logged at flush: "[angle-vrr] passBreaks=N". Expected per frame = the
   number of REGULAR pass ends of such passes (typically 1); more = an encoder
   switch in the middle of a pass (texture upload, mipmap, query).

Host test note: glReadPixels from an imported PRIVATE texture on macOS,
without the iOS feature copyTextureToBufferForReadOptimization, takes the CPU
getBytes path and crashes ("CPU access ... Private ... disallowed") — host
tests therefore read back via Metal blit. Nothing is affected on the device
(feature active; the compositor reads on the Metal side).

4. Load-action probe (KL_ANGLE_VRR_TRACE, mtl_command_buffer.mm): for every
   render pass with a rate map OR layered rendering, counts the load actions
   of color0/depth/stencil from the CONVERTED MTLRenderPassDescriptor and
   prints them once per second next to encode/s:
     [angle-vrr] passLoads/s rate-mapped|layered passes=N color Clear/Load/DontCare=a/b/c depth=... stencil=...
   Purpose: measure a guest's clear hygiene instead of assuming it. A Load on
   a memoryless attachment is either a planned pass boundary (under a rate
   map: double-warped unresolve) or a skipped clear (write mask/scissor at
   glClear time). Expected picture for an eye/multiview pass:
   Clear/Clear/Clear, Load = 0.

5. Frame GPU time without a GL query (KL_MTL_FRAME_GPU_TIME=1;
   mtl_common.h/.mm, ContextMtl.h/.mm): ContextMtl::flush() (= the guest's
   glFlush, one per frame) closes the running TimeElapsed entry and opens the
   next one; the entry sums GPUEndTime-GPUStartTime over all command buffers
   of the frame, like GL_TIME_ELAPSED. Finished frames sit in a ring; the
   guest fetches them via dlsym:
     extern "C" int ANGLEMetalPopFrameGpuTimeMs(double *outMs);  // 1 = value delivered
   Purpose: OVR_multiview forbids an active GL_TIME_ELAPSED query during
   multiview draws (kMultiviewTimerQuery) — this probe measures identically,
   before and after, without deviating from the spec. Do not use together
   with GL TimeElapsed queries (both drive the single active entry of the
   CommandQueue).

